\details{
Ranking the models requires knowing whether the metric is better
when larger or smaller. This is taken from \code{x}, which records it when
\code{\link[=perf_mod]{perf_mod()}} is given a \code{tune_results} object or a workflow set. Fits made
from a data frame, an \code{rset} object, or a \code{caret} \code{resamples} object do not
carry a metric, so \code{compare_to_leader()} cannot be used on them.

Differences are parameterized as \code{model - leader} so that they
describe how much a candidate gives up relative to the current best. Since
the leader is, by definition, the best model in the set, \code{pr_worse} is the
informative one-sided probability. It accounts for the direction of the
metric: for metrics that are minimized (such as RMSE) it is the posterior
probability that the difference is positive, and for metrics that are
maximized (such as the area under the ROC curve) it is the probability that
the difference is negative.

The leader's own row describes a comparison against itself. That difference
is exactly zero, so \code{mean_diff}, \code{lower_diff}, \code{upper_diff}, and \code{pr_worse}
