that beat it have posterior mass above \code{size} in the \emph{better} direction.
That mass is not reported, so \code{pract_equiv} and \code{pract_worse} sum to less
than one for those rows. Their \code{pr_worse} values are still correct, and
\code{1 - pr_worse} is the probability that the model beats the leader.

If a transformation was used when \code{x} was created, the inverse is applied
before the summaries and differences are computed.

Which models compete is decided when \code{x} is fit, not here. For workflow
sets, \code{perf_mod(select_best = TRUE)} collapses workflows that fit the same
type of model with the same engine down to the best of each group, which is
useful when a workflow set contains several preprocessors for the same
model.
